## Why This Is Asked
Tests basic concurrent data structure design and practical use of atomics and memory ordering in a beginner-friendly but realistic setting.

## Key Concepts
- MPSC synchronization using a shared tail and a local consumer head
- Atomic fetch_add for slot reservation
- Memory ordering: release on enqueue, acquire on dequeue
- Correct wrap-around handling in a fixed-size buffer

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-C code (C11 atomics style)
#define N (1<<12)
typedef struct { uint64_t data; uint64_t seq; } slot_t;
slot_t buf[N];
atomic_uintptr_t tail = 0; // producer index
size_t head = 0; // consumer index

bool enqueue(uint64_t v){
  size_t t = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  if (t >= N) return false;
  buf[t].data = v;
  atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
  buf[t].seq = t; // simple wrap-safe signal
  return true;
}

bool dequeue(uint64_t* out){
  if (head >= atomic_load_explicit(&tail, memory_order_acquire)) return false;
  *out = buf[head].data;
  head++;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to detect and recover from corrupted data?
- How would you benchmark throughput under 1000 producers vs 1 consumer?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of lock-free free-lists, memory safety, and practical memory management in low-level code. It also checks defensive programming (double-free) and how to reason about under-concurrency correctness.

## Key Concepts

- Lock-free free-list stored inside blocks; head is an atomic pointer; push/pop via CAS loops
- Alignment and pool boundaries; membership checks to detect invalid frees
- Avoids dynamic allocations; upfront memory footprint predictable
- Stress testing under contention and memory ordering effects

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-C-like representation
typedef struct Block {
  struct Block* next;
  char payload[SIZE];
} Block;
Block pool[N];
_A< Block* > head;

Block* allocate() {
  Block* old = atomic_load(&head);
  while (old) {
    Block* next = old->next;
    if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&head, &old, next))
      return old;
    old = atomic_load(&head);
  }
  return NULL;
}

void free(Block* b) {
  Block* old = atomic_load(&head);
  do {
    b->next = old;
  } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&head, &old, b));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect and prevent ABA in this setup?
- How would you validate correctness with a multithreaded stress test and measure throughput?

